{
  "$comment": "The repository entries of a team's capture policy, as the account service stores them and as terma-cli reads them. Byte-identical copies: terma-cli internal/routing/testdata/entry_grammar.json is the source, and mirador-platform account/api/internal/policy/testdata/entry_grammar.json the copy, which the platform's entry-grammar CI job compares with terma-cli's main. So a change lands here first and is released before the server stores anything new: the CLI that developers run must accept an entry before the server stores it, and a stored form an older CLI refuses or reads differently needs a new policy major. server: how the account service stores the entry (validatePolicyRepository, after trimming): exact, wildcard or refused. cli: how terma-cli's routing.ParseEntry reads it with wildcards on: exact, wildcard or refused. The CLI is the more lenient reader: it also takes clone URLs, which the server refuses. Contract, checked on both sides from these two columns: an entry the server stores is never refused by the CLI, and is a wildcard to both or to neither.",
  "cases": [
    {"entry": "github.com/acme/api", "server": "exact", "cli": "exact"},
    {"entry": "GitHub.com/Owner/Repo.js", "server": "exact", "cli": "exact"},
    {"entry": "gitlab.com/group/sub/app", "server": "exact", "cli": "exact"},
    {"entry": "Localhost/a/b", "server": "exact", "cli": "exact"},
    {"entry": "git.example.co.uk/Données/app", "server": "exact", "cli": "exact"},
    {"entry": "github.com/Acme/*", "server": "wildcard", "cli": "wildcard"},
